I attach a .tar.gz with all the captures I took, the filenames should =20
be self explanatory. If you prefer me to dump all that into the e-mail =20
text field just ask, but it's quite a big amount of data.

Both 3.16 and 3.18 kernels seemed to respond to the same events. Oddly =20
enough I've noticed that although 3 devices for rawhid were listed, =20
only two captured events. Here's the output from hid-replay that shows =20
my devices:

Available devices:
/dev/hidraw0:=09USB Keyboard
/dev/hidraw1:=09USB Keyboard
/dev/hidraw2:=09USB OPTICAL MOUSE
/dev/hidraw3:=09Wacom Co.,Ltd. Bamboo Pad, USB
/dev/hidraw4:=09Wacom Co.,Ltd. Bamboo Pad, USB
/dev/hidraw5:=09Wacom Co.,Ltd. Bamboo Pad, USB

Only hidraw4 and 5 reported events to hid-replay. Also note that =20
hidraw5 corresponds to the touchpad while hidraw5 corresponds to the =20
stylus.

I also just found out that the stylus hardware button is captured by =20
the touchpad device and not by the stylus one (that actually makes =20
more sense as it acts like a mouse button), so all the data I put =20
about the stylus key should just contain stylus movement data. I added =20
a file stylus-key.hid which is actually capturing the clicks for that =20
button.

I should also say that some events, like clicking the screen with the =20
stylus will be mixed with the mouse movement (I tried to avoid that by =20
clicking fast enough but I guess it's impossible to completely avoid =20
it).

PD: I see you're using a gmail address to reply, should I reply to =20
that address better?

